  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Explain the key role of /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow and cron jobs in Linux. (5 marks)', '/etc/passwd contains one line per user account with the username, a placeholder ''x'' for the password, user ID, group ID, comment, home directory and login shell; it is readable by all users because many programs need it. /etc/shadow stores the encrypted (hashed) passwords and password-ageing details such as last change and expiry; it is readable only by root, which protects the hashes from attackers. Cron jobs are tasks scheduled with the cron daemon; each crontab line gives minute, hour, day of month, month, day of week and the command (e.g. ''0 2 * * * /backup.sh'' runs a backup at 2 a.m. daily). They automate routine work such as backups, updates and log cleanup.', null, '[{"point": "/etc/passwd: world-readable list of user accounts - username, UID, GID, home directory, login shell (password field ''x'')", "marks": 2}, {"point": "/etc/shadow: readable only by root; stores hashed passwords and password-ageing/expiry information", "marks": 1.5}, {"point": "cron jobs: scheduled tasks run automatically by the cron daemon at set times (crontab: minute hour day month weekday command), e.g. backups, log rotation", "marks": 1.5}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
