  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'A company with a large open-office setup has deployed several Wi-Fi access points (APs). Employees complain that their Wi-Fi connections drop randomly, especially in high-density areas. Wired connections work perfectly. As the network administrator, identify possible causes and describe five systematic steps you would take to monitor and troubleshoot the Wi-Fi instability. (5 marks)', 'Possible causes: too many clients per access point, channel overlap and interference (neighbouring APs, microwaves, Bluetooth), poor AP placement or power settings, roaming problems, DHCP address exhaustion, or outdated firmware. Steps: 1. Collect information: which users, devices, places and times are affected; review AP/controller logs and client counts. 2. Perform a wireless site survey and spectrum analysis to find weak signal, interference and co-channel overlap; assign non-overlapping channels (1, 6, 11 on 2.4 GHz) and move clients to 5 GHz. 3. Check load on each AP; in dense areas add APs, enable band steering and client load balancing, and reposition APs. 4. Check transmit power and roaming thresholds, DHCP scope size and lease time, and authentication (RADIUS/WPA2-Enterprise) logs. 5. Update AP firmware and client drivers, apply the changes one at a time, then monitor performance and document the fix.', null, '[{"point": "Step 1: gather information - when, where, which devices; check AP/controller logs and client counts", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Step 2: wireless site survey / spectrum analysis for interference and channel overlap (2.4 GHz channels 1, 6, 11; use 5 GHz)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Step 3: check AP capacity/load and placement; add APs or enable band steering and load balancing in dense areas", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Step 4: check power levels, roaming settings, DHCP lease/IP exhaustion and authentication (RADIUS) issues", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Step 5: update AP firmware/client drivers, apply changes, then monitor and document results", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
